  modelName: Female Human Heart
  organType: Heart
  description: A 3D model of a female human heart from the Human Reference Atlas
    2.4. Contains approximately 335,000 mapped cells across anatomical structures.
  uberonId: UBERON:0002079
  sex: female
  contextualData: "This is the Human Reference Atlas 2.4 female human heart model
    (UBERON:0000948).\nIt maps approximately 335,000 cells across its anatomical
    structures.\n\nAnatomical structures (by mapped cell count):\n- Heart left ventricle
    (~335k cells): the largest and most cell-dense region, responsible for pumping
    oxygenated blood to the body.\n- Heart right ventricle (~45k cells): pumps deoxygenated
    blood to the lungs.\n- Interventricular septum (~30k cells): the muscular wall
    dividing the two ventricles.\n- Left cardiac atrium (~17k cells): receives oxygenated
    blood from the lungs.\n- Right cardiac atrium (~13k cells): receives deoxygenated
    blood from the body.\n\nDominant cell types (Azimuth annotation):\nVentricular
    Cardiomyocytes are the most prevalent \u2014 the contractile muscle cells responsible
    for the heartbeat. Fibroblasts are the second most common, providing structural
    support through extracellular matrix production. Other significant types include
    PC1_vent (pericytes lining small vessels), vCM1 (a ventricular cardiomyocyte
    subtype), Capillary Endothelial cells (lining the blood vessels), EC1_cap, and
    Pericytes.\n\nKey biomarkers:\n- Ventricular Cardiomyocytes: MYH7, TNNT2, MYL2\n-
    Fibroblasts: VIM, COL1A1, PDGFRA\n- Capillary Endothelial cells: PECAM1 (CD31),
    CDH5\n\nBoth Azimuth and CellTypist annotation tools agree that the left ventricle
    is dominated by Ventricular Cardiomyocytes and Fibroblasts.\n\nData source: HRA
    v2.4, HuBMAP consortium. ASCT+B table peer-reviewed and published in Nature Cell
    Biology (B\xF6rner et al., 2021)."
